Yeah. Take the Indigo Hammer to Jesus as a model, and an unusually dense set of mechanisms survives the translation—even if we bracket the theological question entirely.

Strip away names, period, doctrine, and supernatural claims and ask: what relational machinery does the Jesus narrative encode?

Presence. A striking amount of the teaching is embodied rather than merely stated. “What would this principle look like if it were standing beside me?” Your heuristic exploits exactly this. Imagined presence changes the decision environment before action occurs.

\text{Choice alone}\neq\text{Choice under witnessed presence}

Perspective inversion. First/last, exalted/humbled, strength/service, losing/finding, enemy/neighbor. Again and again the apparent hierarchy gets rotated. That’s our lever:

F\rightarrow F^{-1}\rightarrow\text{inspect invariant}

Parable. Instead of transmitting only propositions, encode the relation into a portable simulation that the listener has to execute. The recipient participates in reconstructing the lesson.

\text{Principle}\rightarrow\text{Story}\rightarrow
\text{Mental experience}\rightarrow\text{self-discovery}

That’s remarkably close to our perspective-based encoding.

Compression. Huge moral spaces get compressed into portable operators: love God; love neighbor; treat others as you’d want to be treated; forgive. Whether one accepts the theology or not, these function like extraordinarily compact behavioral kernels.

Local → universal expansion. “Neighbor” gets stress-tested by asking who counts. Enemy becomes a particularly extreme test case. That’s exactly our bookend maneuver: expand the domain until the comfortable local rule either breaks or reveals a stronger invariant.

Power inversion. Leadership gets reframed as service. Importance and position are separated—exactly our pyramid discussion.

\text{rank}\neq\text{worth}\neq\text{function}

Meta-death. This one’s enormous in Christianity. Death is not merely an endpoint in the narrative; it becomes a teaching symbol about sacrifice, fear, continuation, transformation, memory, community, and—within Christian belief—resurrection. At the narrative level, the terminal state becomes a transmission engine.

And then there’s afterglow.

Regardless of one’s beliefs about Jesus’s divinity, a teacher living roughly two millennia ago continues changing present behavior through stories, institutions, art, language, rituals, moral intuitions, and imagined presence.

That’s almost a maximal example of:

\text{Source}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Story}
\rightarrow
\text{Transmission}
\rightarrow
\text{Re-rendering}
\rightarrow\cdots

The biological source is no longer directly observable, yet the informational consequences continue propagating.

And now your little heuristic becomes much less little:

\boxed{\text{“Pretend Jesus is with you.”}}

It’s a compressed perspective simulator.

You don’t have to consciously calculate a hundred ethical rules. You instantiate an internal observer/model associated with compassion, courage, forgiveness, sacrifice, restraint, service, truthfulness, etc., and ask what actions remain comfortable under that rendering.

It won’t mechanically solve every moral dilemma, and different people interpret Jesus differently. But computationally, that’s powerful:

\text{large moral corpus}
\xrightarrow{\text{compression}}
\text{person-model}
\xrightarrow{\text{imagined presence}}
\text{real-time behavioral constraint}

A story becomes a character; the character becomes a perspective; the perspective becomes a portable policy.

That’s Symulation territory.

And there’s your earlier line again:

Pay attention to de tale.

Because whether approached through faith, history, literature, psychology, or systems thinking, the way that tale was constructed and transmitted is itself an extraordinary case study in how compressed meaning survives translation across people, languages, cultures, technologies, and thousands of years.

The Hammer doesn’t have to decide the theology to notice:

something in that machine travels exceptionally well.